<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Discovery activity: sign behavior of factored polynomials, built around
     three pre-built Desmos pages (each also has a "Sign Shading" layer
     the instructor added, using 0 < y <= sign(f(x)) and
     sign(f(x)) <= y < 0 to shade above/below the axis) plus a partner
     construction challenge. Prepares for solving polynomial and rational
     inequalities next class; rational functions are deliberately NOT
     touched today and get their own short exploration to open next
     class instead.

     Desmos pages (share links, in order):
       Section 1: https://www.desmos.com/calculator/brkxbkvz0t
                  "One Factor Multiplicity Exploration"
                  f(x) = (x-2)^n, slider n from 1 to 4.
       Section 2: https://www.desmos.com/calculator/kpud68ncgb
                  "Two Factor Multiplicity Exploration"
                  f(x) = (x+2)^m (x-3)^n, independent sliders m, n from 1 to 4.
       Sections 3 & 4: https://www.desmos.com/calculator/5riqcszzk4
                  "Function Sign Analysis Exploration"
                  f(x) = x^2 (placeholder, meant to be replaced by the student).

     Design notes (not shown to students):
     - One controlled variable per stage. Section 1 varies only the
       exponent on a single root; Section 2 fixes both roots and varies
       both exponents independently, so students see that each root's
       cross/touch behavior depends only on ITS OWN exponent, not the
       other root's. A leading coefficient is deliberately absent from
       both of these pages - that variable is withheld until Section 4.
     - Section 3 is a predict-then-check, not another manipulate-and-
       observe page: students must commit to a written prediction before
       opening the graph. The Desmos page starts with the placeholder
       f(x) = x^2 for exactly this reason - remind students out loud to
       type their own formula in place of x^2, not just read the graph
       that's already there.
     - Section 3's polynomial, (x+4)(x-1)^2(x-3)^3, is chosen to break the
       "every root flips the sign" assumption (the multiplicity-2 root at
       x=1 does not flip it) and to show that odd multiplicities above 1
       still cross, just flatter - watch for students who think the
       multiplicity-1 and multiplicity-3 crossings are fundamentally
       different in sign behavior.
     - Section 4's required target needs an overall negative multiplier,
       which nothing before it has modeled directly. The retrieval
       question at the end of Section 3 (multiply by -1 - what changes
       and what doesn't?) puts that tool in students' hands before they
       need it, without hinting that THIS target needs it. If a pair's
       graph is a mirror image of the target (right shape, wrong side of
       the axis), that's the moment to ask what single change would flip
       the whole picture - avoid just telling them.
     - Keep circulating during Section 4 - it is the only paired,
       synchronous moment in an otherwise individually-paced worksheet,
       and it's where a stuck pair loses the most time. -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-polynomial-sign-behavior">
  <title>Sign Behavior of Factored Polynomials</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      When a formula is written in factored form, its graph's sign 
      (positive or negative) is controlled entirely by its roots.
      Today you will use Desmos to discover how. We will use what you notice
      today to solve equations and inequalities later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Work through Sections 1-3 on your own, using your own computer 
      or mobile device. Either scan the QR code or open the link
      shown. After Section 3, compare what you find with your
      neighbor, then work Section 4 together.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-one-root" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: One Root, Changing Multiplicity</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Open the first Desmos page below. It graphs <m>f(x) = (x-2)^n</m>,
        with a slider for <m>n</m>.
      </p>
      <image source="images/qr-desmos-sign-behavior-1.png" width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
        <description>
          <p>QR code linking to the Desmos graph for Section 1.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
      <p>
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/brkxbkvz0t"
             visual="desmos.com/calculator/brkxbkvz0t"/>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-one-root-table" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Set <m>n</m> to each value below. For each one, record the sign
          of <m>f(x)</m> just to the left and just to the right of the
          root at <m>x=2</m>, and whether the graph crosses the axis there
          or just touches it.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>n</m></cell>
            <cell>Sign for <m>x&lt;2</m></cell>
            <cell>Sign for <m>x&gt;2</m></cell>
            <cell>Cross or touch?</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>1</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
          <row><cell>2</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
          <row><cell>3</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
          <row><cell>4</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-one-root-notice" workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What determines whether the graph crosses or just touches at
          <m>x=2</m>? Write a one-sentence rule.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-two-roots" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Two Roots, Two Independent Exponents</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Open the second Desmos page below. It graphs
        <m>f(x) = (x+2)^m(x-3)^n</m>, with separate sliders for <m>m</m>
        and <m>n</m>.
      </p>
      <image source="images/qr-desmos-sign-behavior-2.png" width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
        <description>
          <p>QR code linking to the Desmos graph for Section 2.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
      <p>
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/kpud68ncgb"
             visual="desmos.com/calculator/kpud68ncgb"/>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-two-roots-table" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Try at least three different combinations of <m>m</m> and
          <m>n</m>, including at least one even and one odd value for
          each. Record the sign of <m>f(x)</m> in each of the three
          intervals below.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>m</m></cell>
            <cell><m>n</m></cell>
            <cell>Sign for <m>x&lt;-2</m></cell>
            <cell>Sign for <m>-2&lt;x&lt;3</m></cell>
            <cell>Sign for <m>x&gt;3</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell><m>\phantom{W}</m></cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
          <row><cell><m>\phantom{W}</m></cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
          <row><cell><m>\phantom{W}</m></cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-two-roots-notice" workspace="0.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does changing <m>n</m> ever affect whether the graph crosses or
          touches at <m>x=-2</m>? Does changing <m>m</m> ever affect what
          happens at <m>x=3</m>? What does that tell you about how the two
          roots interact?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-predict-check" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: Predict, Then Check</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Do <em>not</em> open the Desmos page yet. Consider
        <md>f(x) = (x+4)(x-1)^2(x-3)^3</md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-predict" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Before touching Desmos, predict the sign of <m>f(x)</m> in each
          interval below.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Interval</cell>
            <cell>Predicted sign</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell><m>x&lt;-4</m></cell><cell/></row>
          <row><cell><m>-4&lt;x&lt;1</m></cell><cell/></row>
          <row><cell><m>1&lt;x&lt;3</m></cell><cell/></row>
          <row><cell><m>x&gt;3</m></cell><cell/></row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-check" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now open the Desmos page below. It starts with the
          placeholder formula <m>f(x) = x^2</m>. Change the polynomial 
          from <m>x^2</m> to <m>(x+4)(x-1)^2(x-3)^3</m> and check your 
          prediction. Where, if anywhere, were you wrong? Update your
          table above if needed.
        </p>
        <image source="images/qr-desmos-sign-behavior-3.png" width="20%" margins="0% 80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              QR code linking to the Desmos graph for Sections 3 and 4.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
        <p>
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/5riqcszzk4"
               visual="desmos.com/calculator/5riqcszzk4"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-rule" workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use what you have seen in Sections 1-3 to make a single
          rule: given a polynomial in factored form, how do you decide
          whether the graph crosses or touches the axis at each root?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-negate" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose you multiplied your polynomial from this section by
          <m>-1</m>. Which of your answers above would change, and which
          would stay the same?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-construct" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 4: Construct One Yourself (with your partner)</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Compare your rule from Section 3 with your neighbor's rule. Then
        work together to build polynomials with the sign behavior described 
        below. Use the last Desmos page to check your work. A constant
        multiple in front of your factored formula is allowed if you need.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-construct-required" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Build a polynomial, in factored form, with a root at
          <m>x=-1</m> where the graph touches without crossing, a root at
          <m>x=2</m> where it crosses, and a root at <m>x=4</m> where it
          crosses, and so that <m>f(x)</m> is <em>negative</em> for
          <m>x&lt;-1</m>. Check it in Desmos, then write your final
          formula below.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-construct-bonus" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If you have time: build a second polynomial with a root at
          <m>x=-3</m> where the graph touches, a root at <m>x=0</m> where
          it crosses, and a root at <m>x=3</m> where it touches 
          so that <m>f(x)</m> is negative for <m>x&lt;-3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
